<!-- ============================================================
     HollowGraph M365 Calendar C2 Detection Rules
     Threat: HollowGraph is a .NET DLL implant that hijacks a
             compromised Microsoft 365 mailbox and uses the
             calendar as a two-way C2 dead drop. The operator
             plants a calendar event at 2050-05-13 (chosen to
             avoid discovery by the mailbox owner, who will never
             scroll that far into the future) and attaches the
             tasking file to it. The implant calls the Microsoft
             Graph API with a date filter targeting 2050 events,
             reads the attachment, executes the embedded `get` or
             `send` command, then uploads exfiltrated data as a
             new calendar attachment on the same event.

             Commands and exfiltrated data are encrypted with a
             hybrid RSA + AES-256-GCM scheme. HollowGraph carries
             a secondary communication channel via DNS tunneling,
             used exclusively to receive refreshed Microsoft Entra
             ID credentials so the implant can reauthenticate to
             Graph API after token expiry without reaching out to
             an attacker-owned server.

             Because all traffic passes through legitimate
             Microsoft Graph API endpoints
             (graph.microsoft.com), network controls keyed to
             attacker-owned destinations have nothing to flag.
             The covert channel is entirely within the tenant's
             own audit log; detectable only through M365 Unified
             Audit Log analysis or proxy-layer URL inspection.

     Attribution: Group-IB (July 2026 discovery).
             High-confidence overlap with Cavern Manticore, an
             Iranian Ministry of Intelligence and Security (MOIS)
             -linked actor using the Cavern modular backdoor
             framework (shared command syntax and internal
             tasking). Low-confidence overlap with Lyceum (also
             Iran-nexus). Group-IB declined to formally attribute
             the operator pending further evidence.

     Campaign: Active June 3 - July 9, 2026; found on 12+
             machines, ~3 actively communicating with the
             attacker during the analysis window. Small, selective
             footprint consistent with targeted espionage rather
             than opportunistic crime.

     IOC: Calendar event date 2050-05-13 (ISO 8601)
          Graph API filter: ?$filter=start/dateTime ge '2050'

     Source: Group-IB (July 2026), BleepingComputer, The Hacker
             News, The Register (all 2026-07-20)

     MITRE ATT&CK Techniques:
       T1102.002 - Web Service: Bidirectional Communication (M365 calendar dead drop)
       T1071.004 - Application Layer Protocol: DNS (DNS tunnel for Entra cred delivery)
       T1048.001 - Exfiltration Over Alternative Protocol: DNS
       T1573.001 - Encrypted Channel: Symmetric Cryptography (AES-256-GCM)
       T1573.002 - Encrypted Channel: Asymmetric Cryptography (RSA key encapsulation)
       T1078.004 - Valid Accounts: Cloud Accounts (compromised M365 account)
       T1119     - Automated Collection (file staging as calendar attachments)
       T1020     - Automated Exfiltration

     Rule IDs: 104066-104073
     Author: SecOps Engineer (Purple Team)
     Date: 2026-07-20

     Attack chain:
       1. Adversary compromises target M365 account (credential
          theft or phishing; no Graph API vulnerability exploited)
       2. HollowGraph .NET DLL deployed as implant on victim host
          (delivery mechanism TBD per Group-IB report)
       3. Implant authenticates to Microsoft Graph using stolen
          M365/Entra ID credentials
       4. Implant queries:
          GET graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/me/events
              ?$filter=start/dateTime ge '2050-01-01'
          to retrieve the operator-planted calendar event at
          2050-05-13 containing the tasking attachment
       5. Attachment is downloaded, decrypted (RSA+AES-256-GCM),
          and the embedded `get` or `send` command executed
       6. For `send` (exfiltration): collected files are
          AES-256-GCM encrypted and uploaded as a new attachment
          to the same 2050-05-13 calendar event
       7. DNS tunneling (secondary channel) delivers refreshed
          Entra ID tokens; high-entropy TXT record labels carry
          encoded credential material

     Log source prerequisites (network):
       - Proxy / NGFW logs decoded to JSON with fields:
           dst_host, url_path / uri_path, process.name
         Required for rules 104066, 104067, 104073

     Log source prerequisites (Sysmon - Windows):
       - Sysmon EventID 3 (NetworkConnect) - Wazuh SID 61607
         Required for rule 104070

     Log source prerequisites (M365 Unified Audit Log):
       - Microsoft 365 UAL forwarded to Wazuh and decoded as JSON
         with fields: Operation, Workload, ObjectType, ObjectId,
         StartTime, UserId, ClientIPAddress, AppId
         (Wazuh m365 integration or custom decoder)
         Required for rules 104068, 104069, 104072

     Log source prerequisites (DNS):
       - DNS query logs decoded to JSON with fields:
           dns.question.name (or query), dns.question.type,
           dns.answers (or answer)
         Required for rule 104071

     False positive guidance:
       - Graph API calendar access by non-browser: possible for
         legitimate line-of-business .NET apps, scheduled tasks,
         or RPA bots integrating with calendars. Scope with an
         exclusion list of sanctioned app registrations (AppId
         in M365 audit logs). Any process with no known App
         Registration should be investigated.
       - 2050 date in Graph API URL (rules 104067, 104068, 104073):
         virtually zero legitimate FP; no calendar tool generates
         events 24+ years in the future for normal use.
       - DNS TXT query (rule 104071): higher FP rate; email
         infrastructure (SPF/DKIM), ACME cert challenges, and
         some CDN providers generate similar query patterns.
         Correlate with rule 104066/104070 activity on same host.
       - Calendar attachment frequency (rule 104072): legitimate
         meeting invite processors or calendar sync tools may
         trigger; investigate AppId and UserId pair.

     Severity tiers:
       12 - DNS TXT high-entropy query (lower confidence, needs correlation)
       13 - Non-M365-app process accessing Graph API calendar endpoint;
            M365 audit calendar attachment access (single event)
       15 - Graph API calendar URL with 2050 date IOC;
            M365 audit calendar event at 2050-05-13;
            Composite: non-browser process + 2050 date in same event
     ============================================================ -->

<group name="espionage,c2,hollowgraph,graph_api,m365,calendar_c2,iran,cavern_manticore,">

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Network - Non-M365-client process accessing Graph API
       calendar endpoint (T1102.002, T1078.004)
       HollowGraph queries graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/me/events
       (or /users/{id}/events) with a date filter for 2050 events.
       No legitimate non-M365 application on an endpoint should
       be calling Graph API calendar endpoints. Native M365 clients
       (Outlook, Teams, OneDrive) are excluded; all other processes
       contacting this endpoint should be investigated.
       Log source: proxy/NGFW JSON log with dst_host, url_path,
       and process.name fields.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104066" level="13">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="dst_host|network.destination.domain|url.domain" type="pcre2">(?i)graph\.microsoft\.com</field>
    <field name="url.path|uri_path|http.request.uri" type="pcre2">(?i)/v1\.0/(?:me|users/[^/]{1,200})/(?:calendar|events)</field>
    <field name="process.name|src_process|application" negate="yes" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:outlook|olk\.exe|teams|microsoft\.teams|onedrive|lync|skype|msedge|chrome|firefox|iexplore|brave|safari|chromium|opera|vivaldi|edge)</field>
    <description>HollowGraph: non-M365-client process accessing Microsoft Graph calendar API on $(agent.name)  -  process $(process.name) calling /me/events or /users/*/events; HollowGraph uses this endpoint to retrieve operator tasking from calendar event at 2050-05-13 (T1102.002, T1078.004)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1102.002</id>
      <id>T1078.004</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>hollowgraph,graph_api,calendar_c2,network_detection,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Network - Graph API calendar URL containing "2050" IOC
       (T1102.002) HIGH CONFIDENCE
       HollowGraph's specific C2 mechanism queries calendar events
       with a date filter targeting 2050:
         GET /v1.0/me/events?$filter=start/dateTime ge '2050-01-01'
       or with the specific dead-drop date:
         GET /v1.0/me/events/AAMkAD...  (event at 2050-05-13)
       The string "2050" appearing in a Graph API calendar URL is
       a near-certain HollowGraph IOC. No legitimate calendar
       application filters for or creates events 24+ years in the
       future. Zero expected false positives.
       Log source: proxy/NGFW JSON log with dst_host and url_path.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104067" level="15">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="dst_host|network.destination.domain|url.domain" type="pcre2">(?i)graph\.microsoft\.com</field>
    <field name="url.path|uri_path|http.request.uri|url.query|http.request.uri_query" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:2050(?:-\d{2}){0,2}|ge\+['\"]2050|ge%20['\"]?2050|dateTime.*2050|2050.*dateTime|\$filter.*start.*2050|start.*2050.*filter)</field>
    <description>HollowGraph IOC: Microsoft Graph API calendar query with year-2050 date filter on $(agent.name)  -  specific IOC for HollowGraph C2; implant queries events at 2050-05-13 to retrieve operator tasking attached as encrypted file; no legitimate use case for future-dated calendar queries 24+ years out (T1102.002)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1102.002</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>hollowgraph,graph_api,calendar_c2,ioc_2050,critical_ioc,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       M365 Unified Audit Log - Calendar event created/modified
       with start date in 2050 (T1102.002, T1078.004)
       The operator-side action: adversary plants the C2 dead-drop
       by creating or updating a calendar event with StartTime in
       2050 on the compromised mailbox. This is visible in the
       M365 Unified Audit Log (UAL) as a CalendarAPI Create or
       Update operation with StartTime containing "2050" in the
       ModifiedProperties or event body.
       Log source: M365 UAL forwarded to Wazuh (JSON decoded),
       with fields: Operation, Workload, ObjectType, StartTime /
       ModifiedProperties.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104068" level="15">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="data.Operation|Operation" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:Create|Update|Set|Add|HardDelete|SoftDelete)(?:.*Event|.*Appointment|.*CalendarItem)?|CalendarItem.*(?:Create|Update)</field>
    <field name="data.Workload|Workload" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:Exchange|MicrosoftTeams|OfficeSuite)</field>
    <field name="data.StartTime|StartTime|data.ModifiedProperties|ModifiedProperties|data.Parameters|Parameters" type="pcre2">2050</field>
    <description>HollowGraph IOC: M365 Unified Audit Log records calendar event created/modified with 2050 date on mailbox $(data.UserId)  -  operator planting HollowGraph dead-drop event; investigate CallerIPAddress, AppId, and whether the acting UserId is expected to access this mailbox (T1102.002, T1078.004)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1102.002</id>
      <id>T1078.004</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>hollowgraph,m365_audit,calendar_c2,ioc_2050,operator_action,critical_ioc,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       M365 Unified Audit Log - Calendar attachment operation via
       Graph API (T1102.002, T1119, T1020)
       HollowGraph reads its tasking from and writes exfiltrated
       data to attachments on the 2050-dated calendar event.
       This manifests in the UAL as attachment Create/Get
       operations on a CalendarItem via Graph API (ClientAppId
       will show the registered app or the raw Microsoft Graph
       client ID). A single such event is investigable; rule
       104072 escalates on repeated access patterns.
       Log source: M365 UAL forwarded to Wazuh (JSON decoded),
       with fields: Operation, Workload, ObjectType / ObjectId.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104069" level="13">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="data.Operation|Operation" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:Create|Get|Update|Delete|Add|Access).*(?:Attach|Attachment)|(?:Attach|Attachment).*(?:Create|Get|Add|Access|Upload|Download)</field>
    <field name="data.Workload|Workload" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:Exchange|Calendar|OfficeSuite)</field>
    <description>HollowGraph: M365 Unified Audit Log records calendar attachment operation via Graph API for user $(data.UserId)  -  HollowGraph C2 reads encrypted tasking from and uploads encrypted exfil data to attachments on the 2050-05-13 dead-drop event; investigate AppId, CallerIPAddress, and attachment size/name (T1102.002, T1119, T1020)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1102.002</id>
      <id>T1119</id>
      <id>T1020</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>hollowgraph,m365_audit,calendar_attachment,exfiltration,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Sysmon EventID 3 - Non-M365-app .NET process establishing
       TLS connection to graph.microsoft.com (T1102.002, T1059.005)
       Sysmon NetworkConnect events capture the process making
       outbound connections. On a typical enterprise endpoint,
       connections to graph.microsoft.com should come only from
       Outlook (OUTLOOK.EXE), Teams (ms-teams.exe), Edge
       (msedge.exe), or OneDrive (onedrive.exe). A non-M365-app
       process, especially one with a .NET runtime signature
       (clrjit.dll, mscorlib.dll ImageLoad preceding the
       NetworkConnect) is a strong HollowGraph indicator.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104070" level="13">
    <if_sid>61607</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.destinationHostname" type="pcre2">(?i)graph\.microsoft\.com</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" negate="yes" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:OUTLOOK\.EXE|olk\.exe|ms-teams\.exe|Teams\.exe|OneDrive\.exe|lync\.exe|msedge\.exe|chrome\.exe|firefox\.exe|iexplore\.exe|brave\.exe|MicrosoftEdge\.exe|SearchApp\.exe|explorer\.exe)</field>
    <description>HollowGraph: non-M365-client process establishing TLS connection to graph.microsoft.com on $(win.eventdata.computer) by $(win.eventdata.user)  -  process $(win.eventdata.image) contacting Graph API; no legitimate endpoint .NET app should query graph.microsoft.com calendar endpoints; investigate for HollowGraph implant DLL (T1102.002, T1059.005)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1102.002</id>
      <id>T1059.005</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>hollowgraph,sysmon_network,graph_api,m365,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       DNS - High-entropy TXT record query (potential Entra ID
       credential delivery via DNS tunnel) (T1071.004, T1048.001)
       HollowGraph's secondary channel uses DNS tunneling to
       deliver refreshed Microsoft Entra ID credentials to the
       implant after token expiry. This avoids any connection to
       an attacker-owned IP. The DNS TXT query labels carrying
       encoded credential material will exhibit elevated entropy
       and abnormally long subdomain strings compared to
       legitimate TXT queries (SPF, DKIM, ACME challenges).
       Pattern: label length gte 20 chars AND query depth gte 4
       labels (base64/hex-encoded data prepended to attacker
       domain). Correlate with rule 104066 / 104070 on same host
       to raise confidence.
       Log source: DNS query log decoded to JSON, fields:
         dns.question.name (or query), dns.question.type.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104071" level="12">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="dns.question.type|query_type|record_type" type="pcre2">(?i)^TXT$</field>
    <field name="dns.question.name|query|dns.query" type="pcre2">^[a-zA-Z0-9+/=_-]{20,}\.[a-zA-Z0-9+/=_-]{8,}\.[a-zA-Z0-9-]{2,}\.[a-zA-Z]{2,}$</field>
    <description>HollowGraph potential DNS tunnel: high-entropy TXT query from $(agent.name)  -  query label structure consistent with HollowGraph secondary channel delivering Entra ID credentials via DNS tunneling; standalone alert is low-confidence; correlate with Graph API calendar access (rules 104066, 104070) on the same host (T1071.004, T1048.001)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1071.004</id>
      <id>T1048.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>hollowgraph,dns_tunnel,entra_credential_delivery,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       M365 Unified Audit Log - Frequency: repeated calendar
       attachment operations in short window (T1102.002, T1119)
       HollowGraph's automated polling/exfiltration cycle produces
       a burst of attachment GET/POST operations against the
       same 2050-dated calendar event. Legitimate calendar apps
       do not repeatedly read/write event attachments in a 2-minute
       window. Ten or more attachment operations in 120 seconds
       from the same UserId is a strong automation signal.
       This rule fires when rule 104069 is matched 10+ times in
       120 seconds by the same identity (same_field: data.UserId).
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104072" level="14" frequency="10" timeframe="120">
    <if_matched_sid>104069</if_matched_sid>
    <same_field>data.UserId</same_field>
    <description>HollowGraph: repeated M365 calendar attachment operations by same user identity in 2-minute window  -  10+ attachment access events consistent with HollowGraph automated C2 polling cycle; implant automatically checks for updated tasking attachments on 2050-05-13 dead-drop event (T1102.002, T1119)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1102.002</id>
      <id>T1119</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>hollowgraph,m365_audit,repeated_calendar_access,automated_collection,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Composite - Non-M365-client process AND Graph API calendar
       URL with 2050 date IOC in the same event (T1102.002)
       HIGH CONFIDENCE - FULL IR RESPONSE WARRANTED
       When a single proxy log event simultaneously shows:
         (a) a non-M365-app process (negate on known M365 clients)
         (b) a Graph API URL with the HollowGraph 2050 date IOC
       both conditions together meet the threshold for confirmed
       HollowGraph C2 activity. This fires at level 15 and should
       trigger immediate IR response.
       =========================================================== -->
  <rule id="104073" level="15">
    <if_matched_sid>104066</if_matched_sid>
    <field name="url.path|uri_path|http.request.uri|url.query|http.request.uri_query" type="pcre2">(?i)(?:2050(?:-\d{2}){0,2}|ge\+['\"]2050|ge%20['\"]?2050|dateTime.*2050|2050.*dateTime|\$filter.*start.*2050)</field>
    <description>HollowGraph CONFIRMED: non-M365-client process querying Microsoft Graph calendar API with 2050-date dead-drop IOC on $(agent.name)  -  composite match on both non-browser Graph calendar access (rule 104066) and HollowGraph-specific 2050 date filter; RSA+AES-256-GCM encrypted tasking or exfil attachment in progress; full incident response warranted; isolate host and revoke M365 credentials immediately (T1102.002)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1102.002</id>
      <id>T1078.004</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>hollowgraph,graph_api,calendar_c2,ioc_2050,critical_ioc,confirmed_implant,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
